      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Distribution of a Transformed Random Variable",
      "problem": "The random variable $X$ has the following probability distribution.\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ |\n\nLet $Y=2X+1.$ The distribution of $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $y$ | $-3$ | $a$ | $1$ | $b$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $c$ | $0.2$ | $d$ |\n\nWhat is the value of $a+b+c?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's add a $Y=2X+1$ row to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nNow, ${f}_{X}(x)={f}_{Y}(y)$ for corresponding values of $X$ and $Y.$ Therefore, the distribution of $Y$ is as follows:\n\n| $y$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ |\n\nSo, we have $a=-1,b=3,c=0.1,$ and therefore $a+b+c=2.1.$",
